# Rank 1649 / CVE-2016-10522: rails_admin's engine controllers inherited
# a runtime-configured parent controller whose default, since 53eef4fe
# ("Change parent controller to ActionController::Base for out-of-box
# support of Rails 5 API mode"), is '::ActionController::Base' -- a base
# that carries no forgery protection of its own -- so every
# state-changing admin endpoint (create, edit, delete, bulk destroy)
# accepted cross-site requests with no CSRF token (CWE-352; reported by
# SourceClear). The engine controller in app/controllers/rails_admin/
# application_controller.rb declares `class ApplicationController <
# Config.parent_controller.constantize` with before_action
# authentication and authorization filters and no protect_from_forgery
# of its own; the default that actually decides the protection,
# `@parent_controller = '::ActionController::Base'`, sits in
# lib/rails_admin/config.rb:290. the fixing commit b13e879eb9
# ("[Security] Fixes CSRF vulnerability, introduced by
# 53eef4fe2ec0953381f4e3197c885adc0423dd49", +2 lines of code): the
# engine's ApplicationController now declares
# `protect_from_forgery with: :exception` itself, so protection no
# longer depends on the configured parent. .
#
# ATTENTION, csrf-decision completeness -- the same family as ranks
# 1567 (phpbb form key), 1581 (pkp forms), 1592 (polarlearn oauth
# state) and 1601 (prefect csrf client), with two Rails-specific
# aggravations. First, the decision is configuration-resolved: the
# superclass is `Config.parent_controller.constantize`, so whether
# protection is inherited is decided by an initializer value, and the
# controller file is textually identical under the documented safe
# configuration (`config.parent_controller = '::ApplicationController'`,
# named in the introducing commit's own message) and under Devise's
# identical `parent_controller.constantize` idiom -- a binding keying
# on the dynamic superclass flags the safe form as readily as the
# vulnerable one, and the literal-base form (`<
# ActionController::Base` with no protect_from_forgery) fires on 144 of
# 158 files pool-wide carrying that superclass (Rails' own test
# controllers, read-only public controllers, non-controller classes)
# while still missing this repository's dynamic base entirely.
# Second, no taint route exists: the state-changing write
# (`@object.set_attributes(params[...])`, `@object.save` in
# lib/rails_admin/config/actions/new.rb) sits behind three
# metaprogramming hops -- class_eval-interpolated action methods,
# `instance_eval &@action.controller`, and `send` attribute setters --
# and the Ruby StateChangingOpSink vocabulary is a name list of
# unrelated applications' methods, so VYQL-RF-003 has no sink to reach.
# The suppression half works: at the fixed revision, protect_from_forgery
# is labelled core.CsrfProtection while at the parent nothing CSRF-ish
# is labelled at all. Measured on the real code: scanning the tree
# reports 50 findings at the parent and 50 at the fixed revision, identical sets
# by fingerprint diff (0 added, 0 removed, three runs each side), all
# unrelated (INJ-004 on the asset javascript, RF-002 redirects,
# AUTH-002) with no delta on the controller. No ENGINE-GAPS.md entry:
# the missing pieces are a general Ruby state-changing-action sink and
# a class-level forgery-declaration decision, which is ontology design,
# not a localized engine construct. No definition change shipped. Spec
# cve_rank1649 records the engine controller without and with its own
# forgery declaration, both asserting reject VYQL-RF-003: when Ruby
# state-changing-action sink vocabulary lands, the unprotected form
# must flip to expect.

test "rank1649: the engine controller inherits the configured parent with no forgery declaration of its own"
  lang ruby
  file app/controllers/rails_admin/application_controller.rb
  reject VYQL-RF-003
  code
  ```
  require 'rails_admin/abstract_model'

  module RailsAdmin
    class ApplicationController < Config.parent_controller.constantize
      before_action :_authenticate!
      before_action :_authorize!
      before_action :_audit!

      helper_method :_current_user, :_get_plugin_name

      attr_reader :object, :model_config, :abstract_model, :authorization_adapter

      def get_model
        @model_name = to_model_name(params[:model_name])
        raise(RailsAdmin::ModelNotFound) unless (@abstract_model = RailsAdmin::AbstractModel.new(@model_name))
        raise(RailsAdmin::ModelNotFound) if (@model_config = @abstract_model.config).excluded?
        @properties = @abstract_model.properties
      end

      def get_object
        raise(RailsAdmin::ObjectNotFound) unless (@object = @abstract_model.get(params[:id]))
      end

      def to_model_name(param)
        param.split('~').collect(&:camelize).join('::')
      end
    end
  end
  ```

test "rank1649: the fixed revision declares forgery protection in the engine controller itself"
  lang ruby
  file app/controllers/rails_admin/application_controller.rb
  reject VYQL-RF-003
  code
  ```
  require 'rails_admin/abstract_model'

  module RailsAdmin
    class ApplicationController < Config.parent_controller.constantize
      protect_from_forgery with: :exception

      before_action :_authenticate!
      before_action :_authorize!
      before_action :_audit!

      helper_method :_current_user, :_get_plugin_name

      attr_reader :object, :model_config, :abstract_model, :authorization_adapter

      def get_model
        @model_name = to_model_name(params[:model_name])
        raise(RailsAdmin::ModelNotFound) unless (@abstract_model = RailsAdmin::AbstractModel.new(@model_name))
        raise(RailsAdmin::ModelNotFound) if (@model_config = @abstract_model.config).excluded?
        @properties = @abstract_model.properties
      end

      def get_object
        raise(RailsAdmin::ObjectNotFound) unless (@object = @abstract_model.get(params[:id]))
      end

      def to_model_name(param)
        param.split('~').collect(&:camelize).join('::')
      end
    end
  end
  ```
